         "text": [
            {
               "text": "#### The First to Use Their Hands\n\nIn the previous lesson, we saw that we can use our hands to count.\n\nAlthough it is not possible to state that other animals, in the past, used their hands to count, we can at least determine when they were used and why we are the first species to use them for that purpose.",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ],
         "desc": null,
         "next": "7aa8a22b-cc43-443e-bd1a-45ac98346264",
         "prev": "",
         "jumpTo": "",
         "answer": null,
         "score": 1,
         "position": ""
      },
      {
         "id": "7aa8a22b-cc43-443e-bd1a-45ac98346264",
         "audio": "",
         "smile": "thinking",
         "source_menu": [],
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "Let's start with a reflection: if you started digging a hole in the ground, would you only find earth as the hole got deeper?",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ],
         "desc": null,
         "next": "93c341e9-1aa0-4433-8747-029f96f11d9a",
         "prev": "",
         "jumpTo": "",
         "answer": "no",
         "score": 2,
         "position": ""
      },
      {
         "id": "93c341e9-1aa0-4433-8747-029f96f11d9a",
         "audio": "",
         "smile": "happy",
         "source_menu": [
            {
               "type": 1,
               "uuid": "0be2ea18-ab78-4cb1-b3be-1ff0ba4ade95",
               "text": "Museo Nacional de Antropologia - México",
               "page": "https://www.mna.inah.gob.mx/",
               "date_time": {
                  "type": "gregory",
                  "year": "",
                  "month": "",
                  "day": ""
               }
            },
            {
               "type": 1,
               "uuid": "ab1dda1e-95c2-4087-8299-e851e58bdc6f",
               "text": "Original Text",
               "page": "index.html?page=tree&arg=33c83383-b9b5-43ec-987d-68f027f342b2",
               "date_time": {
                  "type": "gregory",
                  "year": "2026",
                  "month": "",
                  "day": ""
               }
            }
         ],
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "#### Expanding Knowledge\n\nMany times we do not see something; that does not mean that what we do not see does not exist or has never existed. It may simply be that we do not know.\n\nMaking holes in the ground — or digging — is something scientists do, and with it we can discover what happened in Earth's past. When, for example, we find bones like ours and some tools next to them, we can conclude that people in the past used that tool.",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<img src=\"https://www.historytracers.org/images/MexicoCityMuseo/HomoSapiens.jpg\" />",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": [],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "*Image taken during a visit to the Museo Nacional de Antropología - México",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": [],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ],
         "desc": null,
         "next": "5c8e1349-2f29-4b3e-80d4-76eb8d7e9786",
         "prev": "",
         "jumpTo": "",
         "answer": null,
         "score": 1,
         "position": ""
      },
      {
         "id": "5c8e1349-2f29-4b3e-80d4-76eb8d7e9786",
         "audio": "",
         "smile": "",
         "source_menu": [
            {
               "type": 1,
               "uuid": "a27bf40a-6cf8-4c9a-8b53-efcd80864c83",
               "text": "Fran Dorey. Homo habilis",
               "page": "https://australian.museum/learn/science/human-evolution/homo-habilis/",
               "date_time": {
                  "type": "gregory",
                  "year": "2020",
                  "month": "10",
                  "day": "02"
               }
            },
            {
               "type": 1,
               "uuid": "e4e24b02-9e06-479a-8f5e-33c6576e79eb",
               "text": "Don Hitchcock",
               "page": "https://donsmaps.com/habilis.html",
               "date_time": {
                  "type": "gregory",
                  "year": "2015",
                  "month": "",
                  "day": ""
               }
            },
            {
               "type": 1,
               "uuid": "ab1dda1e-95c2-4087-8299-e851e58bdc6f",
               "text": "Original Text",
               "page": "index.html?page=tree&arg=33c83383-b9b5-43ec-987d-68f027f342b2",
               "date_time": {
                  "type": "gregory",
                  "year": "2026",
                  "month": "",
                  "day": ""
               }
            }
         ],
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "#### Skillful Humans\n\nAs scientists keep digging, there comes a moment when we no longer find humans like us. But we find humans who lived in the past and also made tools; some of them are known as *Homo habilis*.",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<img src=\"https://www.historytracers.org/images/DonsMaps/img_6652habilissm.jpg\" />",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": [],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "*Image made by Don Hitchcock and available on Don's maps.",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": [],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ],
         "desc": null,
         "next": "f6d79260-e367-4313-94c7-3b1aaa2bf6f1",
         "prev": "",
         "jumpTo": "",
         "answer": null,
         "score": 1,
         "position": ""
      },
      {
         "id": "f6d79260-e367-4313-94c7-3b1aaa2bf6f1",
         "audio": "",
         "smile": "thinking",
         "source_menu": [],
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "#### How Is That Possible?\n\nWhat if the tools found with *Homo habilis* had been made by humans like us?\n\nAs time passes, tools are also modified. Furthermore, the tools found with them are not like ours.\n\nIn other words, due to the condition of the tools, the depth at which they were found and their model, we cannot claim that they were made by humans like us.",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ],
         "desc": null,
         "next": "4af50451-d77e-4285-83c7-7a228fe1a50e",
         "prev": "",
         "jumpTo": "",
         "answer": null,
         "score": 1,
         "position": ""
      },
      {
         "id": "4af50451-d77e-4285-83c7-7a228fe1a50e",
         "audio": "",
         "smile": "",
         "source_menu": [
            {
               "type": 1,
               "uuid": "a4f22e25-5839-407f-a1aa-634521d52b42",
               "text": "Francisco A. González Redondo  et al.",
               "page": "https://doi.org/10.4321/S0211-95362010000100007",
               "date_time": {
                  "type": "gregory",
                  "year": "2010",
                  "month": "",
                  "day": ""
               }
            },
            {
               "type": 1,
               "uuid": "cc8c1467-a6af-4f9c-ba60-ab6df35aba05",
               "text": "Original Text",
               "page": "index.html?page=class_content&arg=7fe341a9-420e-4318-be3e-c7c201d821a8",
               "date_time": {
                  "type": "gregory",
                  "year": "2024",
                  "month": "",
                  "day": ""
               }
            }
         ],
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "#### What About Hands for Counting?\n\nWe have already seen that other animals developed tools, but at no point did we mention them using their hands to count. This is because nothing has been found that could indicate that another animal, besides us, used its hands to count.\n\nBut for our species, long before writing was created on any continent, we were already making marks, like the marks of our hands on a baboon bone, such as the one shown on this screen.",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<img src=\"https://www.historytracers.org/images/ResearchGate/Figura-9-Hueso-de-Lebombo.png\" />",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": [],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "*Image made by Enrique Silván Pobes.",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": [],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ],
         "desc": null,
         "next": "922797ba-64d5-4ebe-b5e1-a85ff21a4df0",
         "prev": "",
         "jumpTo": "",
         "answer": null,
         "score": 1,
         "position": ""
      },
      {
        "id": "922797ba-64d5-4ebe-b5e1-a85ff21a4df0",
        "audio": "",
        "smile": "",
        "source_menu": [],
        "text": [
          {
            "text": "#### Conclusion\n\nIn this lesson, we learned how scientists concluded that other humans also developed tools.\n\nFurthermore, we saw that, until another finding is made, we are the first humans to use their hands to count, and this happened before we wrote.\n\nIn the next lesson, we will do something similar, but this time with the voice.",
            "source": [],
            "date_time": null,
            "isTable": false,
            "imgdesc": "",
            "format": "markdown",
            "PostMention": ""
          }
        ],
